From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:02:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: env.MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE=1 runs the runtime's helper
 threads at utility QoS

The runtime's native helper threads, the JIT-pool warmer (page warming
and the periodic address-space censuses) and the stale-pointer healer,
do no work a frame waits for, yet they are created at the QoS of the
thread that started them and run on the performance cores the game's
threads need. Our RDR2 sample s1 showed the warmer in bursts of 20-37 %
of a core; Madeira measured both on performance cores at ~2.8 GHz in
Horizon Zero Dawn.

With MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE=1 (a runtime key, env.MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE=1) each
moves itself to utility QoS when it starts ([eco-native] logs it), so
it runs on the efficiency cores at their lower clock, ECO on or off. Not
moved: the wineserver thread, the Mach exception thread (it services the
faults guest threads wait on), the samplers (already utility), audio,
the UI. Off by default, as in Madeira.

Madeira-commit: 740473a0a9fbcafc97299bb189de0a66c4b78225
Linear: PLA-364
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-20-rdr2-perf-ports.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c |  1 +
 build/ntdll-unix/thread_ios.c       | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c      |  1 +
 3 files changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
index 59a72d8..8a02347 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
@@ -554,6 +554,7 @@ static void *ios_stale_va_scanner( void *arg )
      * MADEIRA_NO_HEAL=1 reverts to dry-run reporting. */
     int do_heal = getenv( "MADEIRA_NO_HEAL" ) == NULL;
     pthread_setname_np( "wine-stale-heal" );
+    { extern void ios_eco_native_self( const char *who ); ios_eco_native_self( "wine-stale-heal" ); }
     for (;;)
     {
         usleep( 100000 );
diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/thread_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/thread_ios.c
index 4e552ce..b93ad39 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/thread_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/thread_ios.c
@@ -1123,6 +1123,30 @@ static void contexts_from_server( CONTEXT *context, struct context_data server_c
 }
 
 
+/* env.MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE = 1 (opt-in, Madeira's 740473a). The runtime's own
+ * native helper threads -- the JIT-pool warmer (virtual_ios.c: page warming
+ * and the periodic address-space censuses) and the stale-pointer healer
+ * (signal_arm64_ios.c) -- do no work a frame waits for, yet they are created
+ * at the QoS of the thread that started them. With the switch each moves
+ * itself to utility QoS when it starts, so it runs on the efficiency cores at
+ * their lower clock, ECO on or off. Not moved: the wineserver thread, the Mach
+ * exception thread (it services the faults guest threads wait on), the
+ * samplers (already utility), audio, the UI. */
+void ios_eco_native_self( const char *who )
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    int r;
+    if (on < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE" );   /* 1: the runtime's native helper threads run at utility QoS */
+        on = e && *e && strcmp( e, "0" ) != 0;
+    }
+    if (!on) return;
+    r = pthread_set_qos_class_self_np( QOS_CLASS_UTILITY, 0 );
+    dprintf( 2, "[eco-native] %s thread at utility QoS%s (MADEIRA_ECO_NATIVE)\n", who, r ? ": refused" : "" );
+}
+
+
